// Web's own error identity.
//
// There is deliberately one small error type here, not a catch-all WebError
// that rebrands failures the existing modules already report well. HTTP
// failures stay HTTPError, HTML failures stay HTMLError, session and security
// failures keep their own identities, and application code catches them by
// the name that actually describes what went wrong.
//
// WebConfigError covers the one thing genuinely new in v0.15: an application
// whose environment contract is missing or malformed. Its message names the
// key at fault and never repeats the value, so a bad secret cannot reach a
// log through an error string.
WebConfigError: Class<Error> := {
    structure: Attributes := (SuperClass.attributes)
}
